"""Chapter 1 drill items — national income and the accounts."""


def q(s, o, i, sol):
    return (s, o, i, sol)


DRILL_1 = [
    q("National income is best defined as the", ["total money held by a country's citizens", "money value of all goods and services produced in a country in a year", "total value of a country's exports", "sum of all wages paid in a year"], 1, "The money value of everything produced in a year. Money holdings are a stock rather than a flow, exports are one component, and wages are only one of four factor incomes."),
    q("Gross domestic product measures output produced", ["by a country's citizens anywhere in the world", "inside a country's borders, whoever owns the firm", "by government-owned enterprises only", "in the manufacturing sector only"], 1, "Inside the border, regardless of ownership. Production by citizens abroad belongs to GNP, and restricting the measure to one sector or one owner would make it something other than GDP."),
    q("Gross national product equals gross domestic product plus", ["depreciation", "net factor income from abroad", "indirect taxes", "subsidies"], 1, "Net factor income from abroad. Adding depreciation would not change the domestic-national distinction, and the tax and subsidy adjustments take market prices to factor cost."),
    q("A country whose citizens earn substantial income abroad while foreign firms repatriate large profits will usually have", ["GNP greater than GDP", "GDP greater than GNP", "GDP equal to GNP", "GNP equal to national income at market prices"], 1, "GDP greater than GNP. Profits repatriated by foreign firms are earned inside the border but leave the country, and if they exceed what citizens earn abroad the domestic total is the larger."),
    q("National income at factor cost differs from GNP at market prices by", ["the addition of depreciation", "the subtraction of indirect taxes and the addition of subsidies", "the addition of net exports", "the subtraction of transfer payments"], 1, "Remove indirect taxes and add subsidies. That adjustment strips out the tax wedge between what buyers pay and what factors receive; depreciation is a different adjustment entirely."),
    q("The three methods of measuring national income are", ["output, income and expenditure", "saving, investment and consumption", "production, distribution and exchange", "taxation, borrowing and spending"], 0, "Output, income and expenditure. B lists components of expenditure, C lists stages of economic activity, and D lists fiscal instruments."),
    q("The expenditure method of measuring national income adds", ["C + I + G + (X − M)", "C + I + G + (X + M)", "C + I + G", "C + S + T"], 0, "Consumption plus investment plus government spending plus net exports. Adding exports and imports rather than net exports double-counts, and omitting net exports misses the foreign sector."),
    q("The income method adds the rewards to the four factors of production, which are", ["wages, rent, interest and profit", "wages, salaries and bonuses", "rent, rates and royalties", "profit, dividends and capital gains"], 0, "Wages, rent, interest and profit — one reward for each factor. The others are subdivisions of a single reward rather than the four-factor classification."),
    q("Which of the following is NOT included in the measurement of national income?", ["payment for a newly built house", "a pension paid to a retired civil servant", "a doctor's fee paid by a patient", "the value of a new machine installed in a factory"], 1, "A pension is a transfer payment: money moves but nothing is produced. The other three are payments for current output or for newly produced capital."),
    q("The sale of a second-hand car is excluded from national income because", ["cars are not counted as output", "the car was counted when it was first produced", "second-hand goods are always imported", "the transaction is not taxed"], 1, "It was counted when new. Only the dealer's margin, which is a fresh service, enters the accounts; counting the whole resale would double-count the same output."),
    q("Double counting in national income accounting is avoided by using", ["value added rather than gross output", "gross output rather than net output", "market prices rather than factor cost", "nominal rather than real values"], 0, "Value added. Adding gross sales of every firm counts the flour once inside the bread and again at the mill; value added counts each firm's own contribution only."),
    q("A baker buys flour for ₦40,000 and sells bread for ₦100,000. The value added is", ["₦40,000", "₦60,000", "₦100,000", "₦140,000"], 1, "₦60,000 — the ₦100,000 of output less the ₦40,000 of intermediate purchases. The ₦40,000 already entered GDP when the flour was milled, and ₦140,000 adds the two figures together, which double-counts."),
    q("In the circular flow of income, households supply", ["goods and services to firms", "factor services to firms in return for incomes", "taxes to foreign governments", "capital to commercial banks only"], 1, "Factor services — land, labour, capital and enterprise — in return for rent, wages, interest and profit. Households buy goods rather than supply them, and tax is paid to the domestic government."),
    q("Which of the following is an injection into the circular flow?", ["saving", "taxation", "government spending", "imports"], 2, "Government spending enters the flow from outside the household and firm sectors. Saving, taxation and imports all remove money from the flow, so they are withdrawals."),
    q("Which of the following is a withdrawal from the circular flow of income?", ["investment", "exports", "imports", "government expenditure"], 2, "Imports pay foreign producers, so the money leaves the domestic flow. Investment, exports and government expenditure all add to it."),
    q("Equilibrium in the circular flow requires that", ["S + T + M = I + G + X", "S + I = T + G", "C + I = G + X", "S + T = X + M"], 0, "Withdrawals equal injections. The other identities either mix a leakage with an injection on the same side or omit one of the six flows altogether."),
    q("If injections exceed withdrawals, national income will", ["fall", "rise", "remain constant", "become zero"], 1, "Rise. More money is entering the flow than leaving it, so total spending and hence output and income expand until the flows balance again."),
    q("Real national income is national income measured at", ["current market prices", "constant prices", "factor cost only", "the prices of the base year, deflated by population"], 1, "Constant prices — the prices of a chosen base year — so that a change in the figure reflects a change in quantity rather than in prices."),
    q("If nominal GDP is ₦200 billion and the price index is 125, real GDP is", ["₦160 billion", "₦250 billion", "₦175 billion", "₦200 billion"], 0, "₦200 ÷ 1.25 = ₦160 billion. Multiplying instead of dividing gives ₦250 billion, and ₦175 billion is the ₦25 billion adjustment applied as a subtraction."),
    q("A country's nominal GDP rises by 20 per cent while the price index rises from 100 to 120. Real GDP", ["rises by 20 per cent", "is unchanged", "falls by 20 per cent", "rises by 40 per cent"], 1, "Unchanged: 1.20 ÷ 1.20 = 1.00. The entire nominal increase is price, so the volume of output is exactly where it was."),
    q("The GDP deflator is calculated as", ["nominal GDP ÷ real GDP × 100", "real GDP ÷ nominal GDP × 100", "nominal GDP − real GDP", "nominal GDP × real GDP ÷ 100"], 0, "Nominal over real, times 100. The deflator is the price index implied by the accounts themselves, so nominal must sit on top."),
    q("Per capita income is obtained by dividing national income by", ["the labour force", "the population", "the number of employed persons", "the number of households"], 1, "The population. Dividing by the labour force gives income per worker, a productivity measure, and the two differ by the dependency ratio."),
    q("A country has a national income of ₦36 trillion and a population of 180 million. Its per capita income is", ["₦200,000", "₦20,000", "₦2,000,000", "₦2,000"], 0, "₦36,000,000,000,000 ÷ 180,000,000 = ₦200,000. The others are each a slip of one or two places in the decimal point."),
    q("Per capita income is a poor measure of welfare because it", ["is measured in money rather than goods", "is an average that ignores the distribution of income", "cannot be compared between countries", "excludes the service sector"], 1, "It is an average. A country can have a respectable figure and a population that has none of it, so distribution has to be measured separately."),
    q("When nominal income and the price level both double, real income", ["doubles", "is unchanged", "halves", "falls by a quarter"], 1, "Unchanged. A doubling of prices cancels a doubling of nominal income, so what the income can buy is the same as before."),
    q("The multiplier is defined as", ["the ratio of a change in income to the change in an injection", "the ratio of consumption to income", "the ratio of saving to income", "the change in investment divided by the change in consumption"], 0, "The ratio of the change in national income to the change in the injection that caused it. The other options describe the average propensity to consume and to save, and a ratio of two changes that has no economic meaning."),
    q("If the marginal propensity to consume is 0.75, the value of the multiplier is", ["0.75", "1.33", "4", "7.5"], 2, "k = 1 ÷ (1 − 0.75) = 1 ÷ 0.25 = 4. 1.33 is 1 ÷ 0.75, the reciprocal of the MPC rather than of the MPS."),
    q("The multiplier is larger when the marginal propensity to", ["save is larger", "consume is larger", "import is larger", "tax is larger"], 1, "Consume is larger, because a larger MPC means a smaller leak in each round. Larger saving, importing and taxing all reduce the multiplier."),
    q("If the marginal propensity to consume is 0.8 and investment rises by ₦5 billion, national income rises by", ["₦4 billion", "₦5 billion", "₦25 billion", "₦6.25 billion"], 2, "k = 1 ÷ 0.2 = 5, and 5 × ₦5 billion = ₦25 billion. ₦4 billion is the first round of consumption, ₦5 billion forgets the multiplier, and ₦6.25 billion is 5 ÷ 0.8."),
    q("The multiplier effect occurs because", ["the government prints more money", "spending by one person becomes income for another, who spends part of it", "prices fall as output rises", "saving is converted into investment automatically"], 1, "Income is respent. Each round of spending becomes somebody's income, and the fraction of it that is spent becomes income again, so the total exceeds the original injection."),
    q("With taxes, imports and saving all present, the multiplier is", ["larger than in a closed economy without taxes", "smaller than in a closed economy without taxes", "exactly equal to 1", "unaffected by any of the three"], 1, "Smaller. Each of the three is an additional leak from the flow, so more of every round of income stops circulating domestically."),
    q("A transfer payment such as a scholarship is excluded from national income because", ["it is too small to count", "no good or service is produced in exchange for it", "it is paid by government", "it is received by students"], 1, "No output is produced. The money is redistributed, not created, so counting it would inflate the total without any corresponding production."),
    q("Which of the following would cause GDP to understate the true level of economic activity?", ["counting value added rather than gross output", "ignoring unpaid household work", "deflating nominal GDP by the price index", "including government services in the total"], 1, "Ignoring unpaid work. Cooking, cleaning and caring are output but are not transacted, so they escape the accounts and the measured total falls short of the real one."),
    q("Gross domestic product at market prices includes", ["indirect taxes and subsidies", "neither indirect taxes nor subsidies", "only subsidies", "only direct taxes"], 0, "Indirect taxes and subsidies: they are part of what buyers pay, so they sit inside the market-price figure and must be removed to reach factor cost."),
    q("The stage at which a country's income per head stops being a good guide to welfare is when", ["the population is large", "income is very unequally distributed", "the country has a trade surplus", "the exchange rate is fixed"], 1, "Distribution. A high average with a small elite and a poor majority describes lives that the average does not represent, which is why the HDI and the Gini coefficient exist."),
    q("In the circular flow, the government sector withdraws through taxation and injects through", ["exports", "government spending", "saving", "investment by firms"], 1, "Government spending. Exports are the foreign sector's injection, saving is a household withdrawal, and investment is the firm sector's own injection."),
    q("An economy has consumption of ₦400bn, investment of ₦120bn, government spending of ₦150bn, exports of ₦90bn and imports of ₦110bn. Its GDP is", ["₦870bn", "₦650bn", "₦760bn", "₦850bn"], 1, "400 + 120 + 150 + (90 − 110) = ₦650bn. ₦870bn adds exports and imports instead of subtracting, and ₦760bn omits the net export term."),
    q("Which pair is correctly matched?", ["GDP — output by residents anywhere; GNP — output inside the border", "GDP — output inside the border; GNP — output by residents anywhere", "GDP — output at factor cost; GNP — output at market prices", "GDP — real output; GNP — nominal output"], 1, "Domestic means inside the border; national means by residents. The first option reverses the two, and the last two attach distinctions of price basis and of deflation that the two terms do not carry."),
    q("National income figures for developing economies are usually understated because", ["their currencies are overvalued", "a large informal sector escapes measurement", "their populations are too small", "they do not export enough"], 1, "The informal sector. Much production is unrecorded, subsistence or paid in cash, so the official total misses activity that is genuinely happening."),
    q("Real national income grows by 8 per cent while the population grows by 3 per cent. Real income per head rises by approximately", ["5 per cent", "11 per cent", "2.7 per cent", "8 per cent"], 0, "The gap between the two rates — about 5 per cent. Adding them gives 11 per cent, and dividing 8 by 3 gives the 2.7 per cent that comes from treating the two rates as a ratio instead of a difference."),
]
